WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.818
In a factory in Taiwan, a drop of molten
tin is flying through a vacuum at two

00:00:04.818 --> 00:00:07.000
hundred and fifty kilometres an hour.

00:00:07.101 --> 00:00:09.040
It is about thirty micrometres wide,

00:00:09.201 --> 00:00:11.301
smaller than the width of a human hair.

00:00:11.401 --> 00:00:13.769
A laser pulse hits it
and squashes it flat,

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:14.817
like a pancake.

00:00:14.857 --> 00:00:19.539
A moment later, a far stronger pulse hits
the pancake and turns it into plasma,

00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:22.777
about forty times hotter
than the surface of the Sun.

00:00:22.878 --> 00:00:27.368
For an instant, that plasma glows with a
kind of light that barely exists on Earth.

00:00:27.475 --> 00:00:28.894
Then the next drop arrives,

00:00:29.052 --> 00:00:30.839
and the whole thing happens again,

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.785
fifty thousand times every second.

00:00:32.893 --> 00:00:36.073
The drops, the lasers,
the tiny artificial stars:

00:00:36.313 --> 00:00:39.313
all of it exists to draw
lines on a computer chip.

00:00:39.431 --> 00:00:41.481
Lines only a few dozen atoms wide.

00:00:41.587 --> 00:00:44.783
To understand why anyone would
build something this extreme,

00:00:44.943 --> 00:00:46.967
we need one stubborn fact about light.

00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:50.460
By the end, every strange part
of this machine will make sense,

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:52.769
because each one fights that single fact.

00:00:52.873 --> 00:00:56.439
A modern chip is a city of tiny
switches called transistors,

00:00:56.617 --> 00:01:00.243
tens of billions of them, wired
together in stacked layers.

00:01:00.328 --> 00:01:02.052
Nobody builds them one at a time.

00:01:02.366 --> 00:01:03.881
They are printed all at once,

00:01:04.038 --> 00:01:05.025
like a photograph.

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:07.123
It starts with a polished disc of silicon,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.055
called a wafer.

00:01:08.095 --> 00:01:11.236
The wafer is coated with a
thin film called photoresist,

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:14.265
a material that changes
wherever light falls on it.

00:01:14.388 --> 00:01:18.147
Above it sits the mask, a plate
carrying the pattern for one layer,

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:20.708
drawn four times larger
than it will end up.

00:01:20.808 --> 00:01:22.520
Light shines through the mask,

00:01:22.691 --> 00:01:27.198
and a set of optics shrinks the image
four times and focuses it onto the wafer.

00:01:27.305 --> 00:01:29.601
It is a slide projector run backwards:

00:01:29.843 --> 00:01:32.744
instead of throwing a
small picture onto a wall,

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:35.585
it squeezes a big picture
onto a tiny patch.

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:37.867
Where the light lands, the resist changes.

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:41.717
Wash it, etch it, and the pattern
is cut into the layer underneath.

00:01:41.821 --> 00:01:45.675
Then the wafer is coated again and
the next layer is printed on top,

00:01:45.845 --> 00:01:46.806
dozens of times.

00:01:46.846 --> 00:01:49.989
So the size of a transistor
comes down to one question:

00:01:50.217 --> 00:01:52.846
how small a detail can
you project with light?

00:01:52.947 --> 00:01:54.840
The answer depends on what light is.

00:01:55.156 --> 00:01:56.063
Light is a wave.

00:01:56.103 --> 00:01:57.574
Watch a wave arrive at a gap.

00:01:57.879 --> 00:02:01.023
If the gap is wide, the wave
passes almost straight through.

00:02:01.128 --> 00:02:04.612
But if the gap is narrow, close
to the size of the wave itself,

00:02:04.773 --> 00:02:06.863
the wave spreads out on the other side.

00:02:07.185 --> 00:02:09.168
This spreading is called diffraction.

00:02:09.257 --> 00:02:11.739
A chip pattern is nothing but narrow gaps.

00:02:12.094 --> 00:02:16.467
A row of fine lines on a mask behaves
like what physicists call a grating.

00:02:16.573 --> 00:02:19.922
Light passing through a grating
does not just carry straight on.

00:02:20.236 --> 00:02:23.113
It splits into separate beams
that fan out at an angle.

00:02:23.259 --> 00:02:25.692
And here is the key: the finer the lines,

00:02:25.857 --> 00:02:27.239
the wider that fan opens.

00:02:27.325 --> 00:02:29.264
To rebuild the pattern on the wafer,

00:02:29.426 --> 00:02:33.195
the lens has to catch those angled
beams and bring them back together.

00:02:33.289 --> 00:02:35.310
If it catches them, the beams overlap,

00:02:35.462 --> 00:02:37.534
they interfere, and the lines reappear,

00:02:37.685 --> 00:02:38.059
sharp.

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:41.359
But if the fan is too wide and
the side beams miss the lens,

00:02:41.522 --> 00:02:43.479
only the straight beam gets through.

00:02:43.561 --> 00:02:45.244
The wafer then receives a flat,

00:02:45.407 --> 00:02:46.236
even glow.

00:02:46.276 --> 00:02:47.741
The pattern is simply gone.

00:02:47.828 --> 00:02:50.575
So there is a hard limit:
below a certain size,

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.498
a line cannot be printed at all.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:55.145
Engineers write that
limit as a short formula.

00:02:55.252 --> 00:02:58.293
The smallest feature equals
a factor called k one,

00:02:58.475 --> 00:03:02.002
times the wavelength, divided
by the numerical aperture.

00:03:02.089 --> 00:03:03.889
Read it as three knobs you can turn.

00:03:03.999 --> 00:03:08.066
The numerical aperture measures how
wide a fan the lens can collect.

00:03:08.425 --> 00:03:10.639
A bigger opening catches wider beams.

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:12.328
The factor k one is cleverness:

00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:15.969
tricks in the lighting and the mask
that squeeze out a little more.

00:03:16.072 --> 00:03:18.894
And the wavelength is the
size of the wave itself.

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.756
Shorter waves fan out less,

00:03:20.925 --> 00:03:22.562
so they can draw finer lines.

00:03:22.654 --> 00:03:27.084
Every chipmaking machine ever built is an
attempt to turn one of these three knobs.

00:03:27.177 --> 00:03:29.947
For decades, the industry
turned the wavelength knob.

00:03:30.045 --> 00:03:33.763
Mercury lamps gave light at four
hundred and thirty six nanometres,

00:03:33.929 --> 00:03:35.815
then three hundred and sixty five.

00:03:35.912 --> 00:03:39.235
Then came lasers, at two hundred
and forty eight nanometres,

00:03:39.395 --> 00:03:41.432
and then one hundred and ninety three.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.374
And there the wavelength knob got stuck.

00:03:43.475 --> 00:03:46.105
Below about one hundred
and fifty nanometres,

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.195
almost every lens material absorbs
the light instead of bending it.

00:03:50.291 --> 00:03:52.071
So engineers turned the other knobs.

00:03:52.179 --> 00:03:55.499
They filled the gap between the
lens and the wafer with water,

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:57.374
which bends light more than air,

00:03:57.534 --> 00:03:59.569
so the lens could collect a wider fan.

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:01.368
When even that was not enough,

00:04:01.538 --> 00:04:03.295
they split each layer into two,

00:04:03.466 --> 00:04:05.620
three, even four separate exposures,

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.887
each printing only some of the lines.

00:04:07.966 --> 00:04:11.010
It worked, but every extra
pass costs time and money,

00:04:11.176 --> 00:04:13.666
and has to line up
perfectly with the others.

00:04:13.757 --> 00:04:17.585
To keep shrinking, the industry had
to turn the wavelength knob again,

00:04:17.745 --> 00:04:19.127
and by an enormous amount.

00:04:19.219 --> 00:04:21.811
The target was thirteen
and a half nanometres,

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.276
fourteen times shorter.

00:04:23.614 --> 00:04:25.079
Extreme ultraviolet light.

00:04:25.172 --> 00:04:27.773
In nineteen eighty six,
a Japanese researcher,

00:04:27.935 --> 00:04:31.132
Hiroo Kinoshita, showed the
first images printed with it.

00:04:31.262 --> 00:04:35.112
Turning that experiment into a factory
tool took nearly three decades.

00:04:35.209 --> 00:04:37.368
Because at thirteen and a half nanometres,

00:04:37.522 --> 00:04:40.709
almost everything we know about
building optics stops working.

00:04:40.809 --> 00:04:43.812
Problem number one: nothing
glows at this wavelength.

00:04:44.134 --> 00:04:46.279
Not a lamp, and not an ordinary laser.

00:04:46.376 --> 00:04:50.775
To make it, you need matter so hot that
its atoms are stripped of many of their

00:04:50.775 --> 00:04:51.593
electrons.

00:04:51.633 --> 00:04:52.161
A plasma.

00:04:52.201 --> 00:04:53.750
Tin turns out to be ideal:

00:04:53.988 --> 00:04:58.575
highly charged tin ions give off much
of their light in a narrow band around

00:04:58.575 --> 00:05:00.421
thirteen and a half nanometres.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.250
So the machine makes tin plasma,

00:05:02.414 --> 00:05:03.610
one droplet at a time.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:06.849
A generator fires a stream
of molten tin droplets,

00:05:07.037 --> 00:05:09.360
each about thirty micrometres across.

00:05:09.439 --> 00:05:12.944
A carbon dioxide laser, built
by the German company Trumpf,

00:05:13.117 --> 00:05:14.038
waits for them.

00:05:14.078 --> 00:05:16.818
Its pulses pass through
four amplifier stages,

00:05:16.996 --> 00:05:19.438
until the beam carries tens of kilowatts.

00:05:19.529 --> 00:05:23.649
The first, weaker pulse hits the droplet
and flattens it into a thin disc.

00:05:23.742 --> 00:05:24.742
Why flatten it?

00:05:24.896 --> 00:05:26.654
A small sphere is a hard target,

00:05:26.819 --> 00:05:28.852
and most of its tin is hidden inside.

00:05:28.937 --> 00:05:31.002
A wide, thin disc spreads that tin out,

00:05:31.153 --> 00:05:33.217
where the main pulse can reach all of it.

00:05:33.321 --> 00:05:37.734
Then the main pulse hits the disc and
turns it into plasma at around two hundred

00:05:37.734 --> 00:05:38.729
thousand degrees.

00:05:38.769 --> 00:05:40.647
Fifty thousand times a second,

00:05:40.835 --> 00:05:43.839
a tiny star is born and
dies inside the machine.

00:05:43.945 --> 00:05:45.732
And it is brutally inefficient.

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:50.115
Only about five percent of the laser
energy comes out as useful light.

00:05:50.185 --> 00:05:52.224
A large curved mirror, the collector,

00:05:52.381 --> 00:05:55.255
gathers the light and focuses
it toward a single point.

00:05:55.337 --> 00:05:59.027
But every explosion sprays tin
at tens of kilometres per second,

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.717
and even one nanometre of tin
on the collector would ruin it.

00:06:02.803 --> 00:06:06.110
So a constant flow of hydrogen
gas sweeps through the chamber,

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.563
cleaning the tin away
before it can settle.

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:12.602
The result is more than five hundred
watts of extreme ultraviolet light,

00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:15.409
steady enough to run a
factory around the clock.

00:06:15.487 --> 00:06:18.577
Problem number two: this light
is swallowed by everything.

00:06:18.685 --> 00:06:21.155
Glass absorbs it, so
there can be no lenses.

00:06:21.235 --> 00:06:25.245
Even air absorbs it, so the entire
light path has to sit in a vacuum.

00:06:25.353 --> 00:06:26.367
That leaves mirrors.

00:06:26.671 --> 00:06:29.613
But an ordinary mirror reflects
almost none of this light.

00:06:29.695 --> 00:06:31.936
So these mirrors are built from layers:

00:06:32.166 --> 00:06:34.694
about forty pairs of
molybdenum and silicon,

00:06:34.866 --> 00:06:37.395
each pair only about
seven nanometres thick.

00:06:37.491 --> 00:06:41.963
Every boundary between the two materials
reflects only a tiny bit of the light.

00:06:42.303 --> 00:06:44.001
On its own, that is useless.

00:06:44.083 --> 00:06:46.689
But the layers are spaced
at half the wavelength,

00:06:46.848 --> 00:06:50.783
so every one of those tiny reflections
leaves in step with all the others.

00:06:50.857 --> 00:06:53.403
The wave crests line
up, they add together,

00:06:53.572 --> 00:06:56.683
and dozens of weak echoes
become one strong reflection,

00:06:56.853 --> 00:06:58.097
about seventy percent.

00:06:58.185 --> 00:07:00.977
It is the same trick that
makes a soap bubble shimmer,

00:07:01.132 --> 00:07:02.425
tuned to a single colour.

00:07:02.535 --> 00:07:04.725
This is also where the
strange number comes from.

00:07:04.883 --> 00:07:09.106
Silicon starts absorbing strongly just
below twelve and a half nanometres,

00:07:09.277 --> 00:07:12.073
and these mirrors work
best just above that edge.

00:07:12.178 --> 00:07:14.728
Thirteen and a half is
where the mirrors work best,

00:07:14.878 --> 00:07:16.878
and where tin happens to glow brightest.

00:07:16.979 --> 00:07:19.799
These mirrors must also
be smooth beyond belief,

00:07:19.975 --> 00:07:22.207
with bumps of around fifty picometres,

00:07:22.383 --> 00:07:23.969
smaller than a single atom.

00:07:24.062 --> 00:07:26.597
If one of these mirrors
were the size of Germany,

00:07:26.752 --> 00:07:29.442
its biggest bump would be
about one millimetre high.

00:07:29.545 --> 00:07:31.595
Zeiss makes them, in southern Germany.

00:07:31.741 --> 00:07:33.511
Now follow the light through the machine.

00:07:33.651 --> 00:07:37.824
From the collector, it bounces off a set
of mirrors that shape it into an even,

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:39.061
curved slit of light.

00:07:39.175 --> 00:07:40.184
Then it hits the mask.

00:07:40.459 --> 00:07:42.385
And the mask, too, has to be a mirror.

00:07:42.494 --> 00:07:45.113
It is the same stack of
molybdenum and silicon,

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.124
with the chip pattern drawn on top
in a metal that absorbs the light.

00:07:49.224 --> 00:07:51.298
The light strikes it at a slight angle,

00:07:51.458 --> 00:07:56.031
about six degrees, so the reflected beam
can travel on without hitting the incoming

00:07:56.031 --> 00:07:56.321
one.

00:07:56.361 --> 00:08:00.981
Then six more mirrors shrink the pattern
four times and focus it onto the wafer.

00:08:01.065 --> 00:08:02.065
Count the bounces.

00:08:02.263 --> 00:08:03.710
There are around ten of them,

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.155
and each one keeps only
about seventy percent.

00:08:06.238 --> 00:08:09.212
Seventy percent of seventy
percent of seventy percent,

00:08:09.377 --> 00:08:10.305
again and again.

00:08:10.345 --> 00:08:12.185
Of the light that leaves the source,

00:08:12.339 --> 00:08:14.485
only a few percent ever reaches the wafer.

00:08:14.569 --> 00:08:16.884
That is why the source
has to be so violent:

00:08:17.095 --> 00:08:19.409
almost all its light
is spent getting there.

00:08:19.507 --> 00:08:22.963
Even then, the machine cannot
expose a whole chip at once.

00:08:23.309 --> 00:08:25.267
The light only covers a thin slit.

00:08:25.353 --> 00:08:28.030
So the mask and the wafer
move in opposite directions,

00:08:28.179 --> 00:08:30.113
sweeping the pattern through that slit.

00:08:30.217 --> 00:08:32.417
Because the image is shrunk four times,

00:08:32.586 --> 00:08:35.745
the mask has to travel four
times faster than the wafer,

00:08:35.914 --> 00:08:36.865
in perfect step.

00:08:36.905 --> 00:08:41.610
The mask stage whips back and forth with a
ccelerations of more than twenty times the

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:42.550
pull of gravity.

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:44.591
Then the wafer steps to the next patch,

00:08:44.745 --> 00:08:47.720
and the dance repeats, around
a hundred times per wafer.

00:08:47.817 --> 00:08:52.398
Every new layer has to land on the one
beneath it to within about a nanometre.

00:08:52.751 --> 00:08:53.867
A handful of atoms.

00:08:53.971 --> 00:08:57.971
A modern machine does all of this for
around two hundred wafers every hour.

00:08:58.067 --> 00:09:00.933
You might think a shorter wave
simply means sharper lines.

00:09:01.229 --> 00:09:02.267
But there is a catch.

00:09:02.345 --> 00:09:05.529
Light arrives in tiny packets
of energy called photons,

00:09:05.702 --> 00:09:08.655
and a shorter wave packs
more energy into each one.

00:09:08.776 --> 00:09:14.408
An extreme ultraviolet photon carries abou
t fourteen times the energy of the deep

00:09:14.408 --> 00:09:16.606
ultraviolet photons used before.

00:09:16.692 --> 00:09:18.293
So for the same total dose,

00:09:18.471 --> 00:09:21.672
the wafer receives about
fourteen times fewer photons.

00:09:21.768 --> 00:09:23.389
And photons arrive at random,

00:09:23.557 --> 00:09:26.408
like the first raindrops
of a shower on a pavement.

00:09:26.494 --> 00:09:31.546
At a typical dose, a square of resist ten
nanometres on a side catches only around

00:09:31.546 --> 00:09:32.794
two thousand of them.

00:09:32.894 --> 00:09:34.400
Some patches catch a few more,

00:09:34.551 --> 00:09:35.434
some a few less.

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:38.478
Where too few land, a line
comes out thin or broken.

00:09:38.812 --> 00:09:41.704
Where too many land, two
lines can merge into one.

00:09:41.801 --> 00:09:43.969
Engineers call these stochastic effects,

00:09:44.132 --> 00:09:47.221
and they are now one of the
hardest limits in chipmaking.

00:09:47.305 --> 00:09:50.125
The cure is more photons:
a brighter source,

00:09:50.305 --> 00:09:51.565
or a longer exposure.

00:09:51.925 --> 00:09:53.965
And every extra photon costs time.

00:09:54.077 --> 00:09:56.877
That is why the race for more
source power never stops.

00:09:56.979 --> 00:09:58.392
With the wavelength fixed,

00:09:58.555 --> 00:10:01.379
one knob is left to turn:
the numerical aperture.

00:10:01.481 --> 00:10:06.320
Today's standard machines collect light
with a numerical aperture of zero point

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.131
three three, enough for lines
about thirteen nanometres wide.

00:10:10.217 --> 00:10:13.685
The newest generation opens
that to zero point five five,

00:10:13.867 --> 00:10:16.057
for lines of about eight nanometres.

00:10:16.137 --> 00:10:18.485
Catching a wider fan means bigger mirrors,

00:10:18.653 --> 00:10:20.387
and steeper angles at the mask.

00:10:20.489 --> 00:10:24.858
So these machines shrink the image eight
times one way and four times the other,

00:10:25.022 --> 00:10:26.879
giving up half the exposure field.

00:10:26.964 --> 00:10:29.674
The first commercial one
was assembled at Intel,

00:10:29.843 --> 00:10:33.344
and each costs around three
hundred and sixty million dollars.

00:10:33.438 --> 00:10:36.243
Only one company in the
world sells these machines:

00:10:36.463 --> 00:10:37.728
the Dutch company ASML.

00:10:37.833 --> 00:10:40.013
So look back at that flying drop of tin.

00:10:40.127 --> 00:10:44.265
The tin, the laser and the plasma exist
because nothing else glows at this

00:10:44.265 --> 00:10:44.908
wavelength.

00:10:44.948 --> 00:10:48.818
The vacuum and the layered mirrors exist
because everything else swallows it.

00:10:48.959 --> 00:10:53.783
The scanning, the speed and the nanometre
precision exist because the light can only

00:10:53.783 --> 00:10:54.507
fill a slit.

00:10:54.547 --> 00:10:59.414
And the endless hunger for power exists
because each photon carries so much energy

00:10:59.414 --> 00:11:00.587
that too few arrive.

00:11:00.681 --> 00:11:03.463
All of it traces back to
that one stubborn fact:

00:11:03.695 --> 00:11:08.101
light is a wave, and a wave cannot
draw a detail much smaller than itself.

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:11.860
This machine is what it looks like
when people refuse to accept that.

00:11:11.976 --> 00:11:14.017
The next time you pick up your phone,

00:11:14.182 --> 00:11:18.760
remember: somewhere inside it are lines
drawn by a star smaller than a grain of

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:19.760
dust.
